\title{Tropical Order Statistics of Seed Distributions:\\
The Median as a Max-of-Mins Polynomial, and the\\
Commutation of Aggregation with Threshold Reading}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{18 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop the median of an odd sample as an object of tropical algebra and derive from that
normal form a group of structural theorems about how noisy threshold experiments should be
summarised. Our starting point is the identity
\[
  \tmed(x_0,\dots,x_{2k}) \;=\; \bigvee_{\substack{S \subseteq \{0,\dots,2k\}\\ |S| = k+1}}\ \bigwedge_{i \in S} x_i ,
\]
valid in any linear order, which exhibits the median as a homogeneous tropical polynomial of
degree $k+1$ in $2k+1$ variables over the bounded tropical semiring $(\vee,\wedge)=(\max,\min)$.
From it we obtain \emph{threshold duality}: $v \le \tmed(x)$ holds iff at least $k+1$ coordinates
satisfy $v \le x_i$, and dually. Threshold duality is the engine of every result that follows.

Our principal theorem is a commutation law between aggregation and threshold reading. Given a
finite grid $G$, a bar $\beta$, and $2k+1$ non-decreasing \emph{retention curves} $c_i$ with
\emph{knees} $K_i = \min\{t \in G : c_i(t)\ge\beta\}$, the pointwise median curve
$t \mapsto \tmed(c_0(t),\dots,c_{2k}(t))$ has knee exactly $\tmed(K_0,\dots,K_{2k})$: aggregating
first and reading the knee second agrees with reading knees first and aggregating second. The
corresponding statement is false for the arithmetic mean (three monotone step curves with knees
$1,2,3$ have mean-curve knee $3 \ne 2$), and monotonicity of the curves cannot be dropped.

We complement this with an axiomatic characterisation: a ternary aggregator on $\mathbb{R}$ that
is monotone, symmetric, conservative, translation-equivariant and self-dual under order reversal
is necessarily the median; the tropical axiom (translation equivariance) is indispensable,
witnessed by an explicit \emph{sum-sign aggregator} satisfying the other four. We prove
nonexpansiveness and a breakdown theorem, whence a \emph{pipeline} breakdown theorem: corrupting
up to $k$ of $2k+1$ seeds, curves and all, cannot move the knee of the median curve outside the
interval spanned by the surviving seeds, whereas the mean pipeline has breakdown point $0$.

We apply the theory to a measured two-context, six-seed attention-budget dataset, deriving the
seed-3 knee $k^{*}=160$ with margin $0.001$; that all four pre-registered point predictions
$\{192,224,240,256\}$ clear the bar yet none is the knee; that the three-seed knee multiset
$\{160,224,256\}$ has median $224 = \tfrac78\cdot d\,\ctx/32$, replicating $112 = \tfrac78\cdot128$
at half the context; that $7/8$ is the unique constant reproducing both rows while the mean admits
none; and that the exact stability region of the reported centre is the ray $x \le 224$, refuting
the informal claim that only a third seed of $256$ or more could move it.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} tropical semiring; median; order statistics; max-of-mins normal form;
threshold duality; knee detection; breakdown point; robust aggregation; attention budget.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The empirical problem}

Many experiments have the following shape. A system has a tunable budget $k$ drawn from a finite
grid $G$; a quality measure $c(k)$ increases with $k$; and the number one wants to report is not a
value of $c$ but the smallest budget at which $c$ clears a pre-agreed bar $\beta$. Call it the
\emph{knee}. Sparse attention budgets, quantisation bit-widths, sample-complexity thresholds,
sensor counts and dosage ladders all fit this template.

Two features create trouble. First, the knee is an \emph{extreme} functional of the curve: it is
determined by where a single crossing happens, and if the curve crosses shallowly, one part in a
thousand of measurement noise can move it by a whole grid step. Second, such experiments are
repeated across random seeds, and one must decide how to summarise the several knees obtained --
or whether instead to aggregate the \emph{curves} and read a single knee from the aggregate.

The dataset motivating this paper makes both problems vivid. In a long-context sequence-modelling
experiment at model width $d=4$ and context length $\ctx=2048$, with retention bar $0.98$
(accuracy at budget $k$ relative to full-context accuracy), three random seeds produced knees
$256$, $224$ and $160$; the third cleared the bar by $0.001$. Four pre-registered point predictions
all failed. Yet the \emph{median} of the three knees was exactly $224$, matching a structural
prediction $\tfrac78 P$ with $P = d\,\ctx/32 = 256$, and matching the same prediction at half the
context, where the knees $\{96,112,128\}$ have median $112 = \tfrac78\cdot128$.

\subsection{The tropical viewpoint}

On any linearly ordered set, $(\vee,\wedge) = (\max,\min)$ forms a commutative idempotent
semiring: the \emph{bounded tropical semiring}. Polynomials over it are the lattice polynomials;
over $\mathbb{R}$ they are the continuous piecewise-linear functions built from coordinates by
$\max$ and $\min$.

Our organising observation is that the median of an odd sample is a tropical polynomial in a
strong sense -- homogeneous of degree $k+1$ -- and that essentially every desirable property of
the median is a shadow of that algebraic fact. Monotonicity is nonnegativity of the coefficients;
translation equivariance is tropical homogeneity of degree one; self-duality under $x \mapsto -x$
is the exchange of the max-of-mins and min-of-maxes normal forms; $1$-Lipschitzness for the
sup-norm follows from the previous two; and the majority-vote behaviour under thresholding is what
makes aggregation commute with knee reading.

\subsection{Contributions}
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Normal form} (\S\ref{sec:normal}): the counting median equals the max-of-mins
tropical polynomial of degree $k+1$, in any linear order.
\item \textbf{Threshold duality} (\S\ref{sec:dual}): thresholding a median is a majority vote.
\item \textbf{Equivariance} (\S\ref{sec:equi}): the median is preserved by every order-preserving
\emph{and} every order-reversing reparametrisation; extremes are exchanged.
\item \textbf{Axiomatic characterisation} (\S\ref{sec:axioms}): five axioms force the median, and
the tropical one is independent.
\item \textbf{Commutation} (\S\ref{sec:comm}) of aggregation with knee reading, for three and for
$2k+1$ seeds; failure for the mean; necessity of monotonicity.
\item \textbf{Robustness} (\S\ref{sec:robust}): nonexpansiveness, breakdown, pipeline breakdown,
and the mean's breakdown point $0$.
\item \textbf{Application} (\S\ref{sec:app}) to the measured dataset.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Definitions}

Throughout, $\alpha$ denotes a linearly ordered set; $\vee,\wedge$ denote $\max,\min$.

\begin{definition}[Median, counting form]
Let $s$ be a multiset over $\alpha$ with $|s| = 2k+1$. An element $m$ is a \emph{median} of $s$ if
\[
  m \in s, \qquad \#\{x \in s : x \le m\} \ge k+1, \qquad \#\{x \in s : m \le x\} \ge k+1 .
\]
\end{definition}

The counting form is the right definition in an arbitrary linear order: it refers to no sorting,
arithmetic or metric.

\begin{definition}[Tropical median polynomial]
For $x : \{0,\dots,2k\} \to \alpha$ set
$\tmed(x) := \bigvee_{|S| = k+1} \bigwedge_{i \in S} x_i$,
the join over all $(k+1)$-element index sets of the meet there. For $k=1$ write
\[
  \med_3(a,b,c) := (a\wedge b)\vee(b\wedge c)\vee(a\wedge c).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Retention curve, bar, knee]
Let $G \subseteq \mathbb{N}$ be a finite grid, $V$ a linearly ordered set of quality values,
$\beta \in V$ a \emph{bar}, and $c : \mathbb{N}\to V$ a \emph{retention curve}. We write
$\IsKnee_{G,\beta}(c,k)$ if
\[
  k \in G, \qquad \beta \le c(k), \qquad \forall j \in G:\ \beta \le c(j) \Rightarrow k \le j .
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Product point, ratio, speed-up]
For an experiment at width $d$ and context $\ctx$, the \emph{product point} is $P = d\,\ctx/32$;
the \emph{ratio} of a knee is $k^{*}/P$; the \emph{deployment speed-up} is $\ctx/k^{*}$.
\end{definition}

\section{The median is a tropical polynomial}\label{sec:normal}

\begin{theorem}[Tropical normal form]\label{thm:nf}
If $m$ is a median of the indexed sample $x : \{0,\dots,2k\}\to\alpha$, then $\tmed(x) = m$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$(\le)$ Fix $S$ with $|S| = k+1$. Since $\#\{i : x_i \le m\}\ge k+1$ in a universe of size $2k+1$,
we have $\#\{i : m < x_i\} \le k$, so $S$ cannot consist entirely of indices with $x_i > m$; pick
$i \in S$ with $x_i \le m$, whence $\bigwedge_S x \le m$. Join over $S$.
$(\ge)$ Choose $S_0$ of size $k+1$ inside $\{i : m \le x_i\}$; then
$m \le \bigwedge_{S_0} x \le \tmed(x)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Three-seed normal form]
$\med_3(a,b,c)$ is the median of $\{a,b,c\}$, and
$\med_3(a,b,c) = (a\vee b)\wedge(b\vee c)\wedge(a\vee c)$. The equality of the max-of-mins and
min-of-maxes forms is the self-duality of the median under order reversal.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proposition}[Conservativity]
$\tmed(x) \in \{x_0,\dots,x_{2k}\}$; in particular $\med_3(a,b,c)\in\{a,b,c\}$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Uniqueness]
A multiset of odd size $2k+1$ has at most one median.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $m<m'$ were both medians, the predicates $x\le m$ and $m'\le x$ are mutually exclusive, so
their filtered cardinalities sum to at most $2k+1$; but each is $\ge k+1$, giving $2k+2\le 2k+1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Existence]
Every multiset of odd size $2k+1$ over a linear order has a median, namely the entry at index $k$
of a sorted representative.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For a sorted list $\ell$, at least $i+1$ entries are $\le \ell_i$ (the prefix of length $i+1$) and
at least $|\ell|-i$ are $\ge \ell_i$ (the suffix). Take $i=k$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Threshold duality}\label{sec:dual}

\begin{theorem}[Threshold duality]\label{thm:dual}
For every $x$ and $v$,
\[
  v \le \tmed(x) \iff \#\{i : v \le x_i\}\ge k+1,
  \qquad
  \tmed(x) \le v \iff \#\{i : x_i \le v\}\ge k+1 .
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
First form, $(\Rightarrow)$: a finite join is attained, so $v \le \bigwedge_{S_0} x$ for some
$S_0$ of size $k+1$, and every $i \in S_0$ satisfies $v \le x_i$. $(\Leftarrow)$: choose $S_0$
inside $\{i : v \le x_i\}$. Second form, $(\Leftarrow)$: any $(k+1)$-set must meet
$\{i : x_i \le v\}$ inside a universe of size $2k+1$, so every meet is $\le v$. $(\Rightarrow)$:
contrapositively, if fewer than $k+1$ indices satisfy $x_i \le v$ then at least $k+1$ satisfy
$x_i > v$, and the meet over such a set strictly exceeds $v$ while being $\le\tmed(x)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Majority vote]
$v \le \med_3(a,b,c)$ iff at least two of $a,b,c$ are $\ge v$; and $\med_3(a,b,c)\le v$ iff at
least two are $\le v$.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{Tropical algebra of the polynomial}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:alg}
Over a linearly ordered abelian group:
\emph{(monotonicity)} $x \le y$ pointwise implies $\tmed(x)\le\tmed(y)$;
\emph{(tropical homogeneity of degree one)} $\tmed(x+t) = \tmed(x)+t$;
\emph{(self-duality)} $\tmed(-x) = -\tmed(x)$;
\emph{(bounds)} $\bigwedge_i x_i \le \tmed(x) \le \bigvee_i x_i$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Nonexpansiveness]\label{thm:lip}
For real samples $\tmed$ is $1$-Lipschitz for the sup-norm; for three arguments,
\[
  |\med_3(a,b,c) - \med_3(a',b',c')| \le \max(|a-a'|,|b-b'|,|c-c'|).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Let $\delta$ be the right-hand side; then $a \le a'+\delta$ etc., so by monotonicity and
homogeneity $\med_3(a,b,c) \le \med_3(a',b',c')+\delta$, and symmetrically.
\end{proof}

\section{Equivariance under changes of coordinates}\label{sec:equi}

\begin{theorem}[Monotone equivariance]\label{thm:mono}
Let $m$ be the median of $s$ with $|s|=2k+1$, and let $f$ satisfy $f(x)\le f(y)\iff x\le y$ on $s$.
Then $f(m)$ is the median of $f(s)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Antitone equivariance]\label{thm:anti}
Under the same hypotheses with $f(x)\le f(y)\iff y\le x$ on $s$, $f(m)$ is again the median of
$f(s)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch for both]
Filtering commutes with mapping: $\#\{z \in f(s) : z \le f(m)\} = \#\{x\in s : f(x)\le f(m)\}$,
which the hypothesis identifies with $\#\{x\in s: x\le m\}$ in the monotone case and with
$\#\{x \in s : m \le x\}$ in the antitone case. Both counting bounds are met, with the two roles
exchanged in the antitone case.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Extremes are exchanged]\label{prop:extreme}
If $M$ is the greatest element of $s$ and $f$ is antitone on $s$, then $f(M)$ is the least element
of $f(s)$.
\end{proposition}

Consequently the \emph{median} speed-up is the speed-up of the median knee, whereas the
\emph{guaranteed} speed-up is the speed-up of the \emph{largest} knee: median and guarantee are
governed by different order statistics, and only the former is coordinate-free.

\section{An axiomatic classification}\label{sec:axioms}

Let $F : \mathbb{R}^3 \to \mathbb{R}$. Consider:
\begin{description}
\item[(M)] \emph{Monotone}: $a\le a'$, $b\le b'$, $c\le c'$ imply $F(a,b,c)\le F(a',b',c')$.
\item[(S)] \emph{Symmetric}: $F$ is $S_3$-invariant.
\item[(C)] \emph{Conservative}: $F(a,b,c)\in\{a,b,c\}$.
\item[(T)] \emph{Translation-equivariant}: $F(a+t,b+t,c+t) = F(a,b,c)+t$ (tropical homogeneity of
degree one).
\item[(D)] \emph{Self-dual}: $F(-a,-b,-c) = -F(a,b,c)$.
\end{description}

\begin{theorem}[Characterisation of the median]\label{thm:char}
If $F$ satisfies (M), (S), (C), (T), (D), then $F = \med_3$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
\emph{(i) Dual identity.} By (D), $F(0,0,-d) = -F(0,0,d)$; by (T) with shift $d$,
$F(d,d,0) = F(0,0,-d)+d$; by (S), $F(d,d,0) = F(0,d,d)$. Hence $F(0,0,d) = d - F(0,d,d)$.
\emph{(ii) Majority.} For $d\ge0$, (M) gives $F(0,0,d)\le F(0,d,d)$, so with (i)
$2F(0,0,d)\le d$; by (C) the value is $0$ or $d$, and $d$ forces $d=0$. So $F(0,0,d)=0$: two equal
votes win. Translating, $F(a,a,c)=a$ and $F(a,c,c)=c$ for $a\le c$.
\emph{(iii) Squeeze.} For $a\le b\le c$, (M) gives $F(a,b,b)\le F(a,b,c)\le F(b,b,c)$, i.e.
$b \le F(a,b,c)\le b$; (S) extends to all triples.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}
$\med_3$ satisfies all five axioms, so Theorem~\ref{thm:char} is not vacuous.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Independence of the tropical axiom]\label{thm:indep}
Define the \emph{sum-sign aggregator}
\[
  \sumsign(a,b,c) = \begin{cases}
    \max(a,b,c), & a+b+c>0,\\
    \min(a,b,c), & a+b+c<0,\\
    \med_3(a,b,c), & a+b+c=0 .
  \end{cases}
\]
Then $\sumsign$ satisfies (M), (S), (C), (D) but not (T), and $\sumsign(0,0,1) = 1 \ne 0 = \med_3(0,0,1)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Symmetry and conservativity are immediate. Self-duality: negation flips the sign of the sum,
exchanging the first two branches, and $\max(-x) = -\min(x)$, $\med_3(-x) = -\med_3(x)$.
Monotonicity is a branch analysis using $\min(x)\le\sumsign(x)\le\max(x)$ in every branch together with
monotonicity of the sum. Translation equivariance fails: $\sumsign(-1,-1,0) = -1$ while
$\sumsign(0,0,1)-1 = 0$.
\end{proof}

Order-theoretic axioms alone do not single out the median; the additional requirement that pins it
down is exactly the tropical one, invariance under a shift of the origin of the measurement scale.

\section{The commutation theorem}\label{sec:comm}

Two pipelines compute a consensus knee from $2k+1$ seeds. \emph{Pipeline A} reads knees $K_i$ from
each curve and reports $\tmed(K)$. \emph{Pipeline B} forms the pointwise aggregate curve and
reports its knee. Pipeline B produces an operating point of an actual aggregate model; Pipeline A
is what practitioners do.

\begin{theorem}[Median--knee commutation, three seeds]\label{thm:comm3}
Let $c_0,c_1,c_2 : \mathbb{N}\to V$ be non-decreasing with $\IsKnee_{G,\beta}(c_i,K_i)$. Then
\[
  \IsKnee_{G,\beta}\bigl(t\mapsto \med_3(c_0(t),c_1(t),c_2(t)),\ \med_3(K_0,K_1,K_2)\bigr).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $K = \med_3(K_0,K_1,K_2)$. \emph{Grid membership}: by conservativity $K \in \{K_0,K_1,K_2\}
\subseteq G$. \emph{Clearing}: by the dual duality applied to $K \le K$, at least two knees are
$\le K$, say $K_i,K_j$; monotonicity gives $\beta \le c_i(K_i)\le c_i(K)$ and likewise for $j$, so
a majority of curves clears the bar at $K$ and by the upper duality the median curve does.
\emph{Minimality}: if $j \in G$ and $\beta \le \med_3(c_0(j),c_1(j),c_2(j))$, then a majority of
curves clears at $j$, so a majority of knees is $\le j$, so $K \le j$.
\end{proof}

Both halves of the proof are the same majority statement read through the two halves of threshold
duality, with monotonicity supplying the equivalence ``curve $i$ clears at $t$'' $\iff K_i \le t$.

\begin{theorem}[General odd case]\label{thm:commgen}
With $2k+1$ non-decreasing curves $c_i$ and knees $K_i$,
$\IsKnee_{G,\beta}\bigl(t\mapsto\tmed(c_0(t),\dots,c_{2k}(t)),\ \tmed(K)\bigr)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Monotonicity makes the index sets $\{i : \beta \le c_i(t)\}$ and $\{i : K_i \le t\}$ coincide;
apply Theorem~\ref{thm:dual} to each side of the resulting majority condition.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Uniqueness of knees]
A curve has at most one knee on a given grid at a given bar, so
Theorems~\ref{thm:comm3}--\ref{thm:commgen} determine the knee of the median curve.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Failure for the arithmetic mean]\label{thm:meanfail}
Let $\sigma_a(t) = \mathbb{1}[t\ge a]$. On $G=\{1,2,3\}$ at bar $1$, the monotone curves
$\sigma_1,\sigma_2,\sigma_3$ have knees $1,2,3$ with median $2$, while their mean curve takes the
values $\tfrac13,\tfrac23,1$ and thus has knee $3 \ne 2$.
\end{theorem}

The mean curve can clear a bar of $1$ only when every component does, so in this configuration the
mean pipeline computes the \emph{maximum} of the knees: a worst-case, not a central, summary.

\begin{theorem}[Monotonicity cannot be dropped]
There are curves on $G=\{1,2,3\}$ at bar $1$ with knees $1,2,3$, two of them monotone, whose
median curve does not have knee $2$: take $c_0(t)=1$ for $t\ne2$ and $c_0(2)=0$, together with
$\sigma_2,\sigma_3$. At $t=2$ the values are $(0,1,0)$ with median $0<1$.
\end{theorem}

\section{Robustness}\label{sec:robust}

\begin{theorem}[Breakdown theorem]\label{thm:break}
Let $x,y$ agree on an index set $T$ with $|T|\ge k+1$. Then
$\min_{i\in T} x_i \le \tmed(y)\le \max_{i\in T}x_i$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
With $v=\min_{i\in T}x_i$, at least $|T|\ge k+1$ coordinates of $y$ are $\ge v$, so by threshold
duality $v \le \tmed(y)$; the upper bound is dual.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The mean has breakdown point zero]
For every $B$ there is $y$ agreeing with the clean data $x\equiv0$ on the majority $\{0,1\}$ with
$\tmed(y)=0$ but $\tfrac13(y_0+y_1+y_2)>B$; take $y_2 = 3B+3$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Pipeline breakdown]\label{thm:pipe}
Let $c'_0,\dots,c'_{2k}$ be non-decreasing with knees $K'_i$ agreeing with a clean vector $K$ on a
set $T$ with $|T|\ge k+1$. Then the pointwise median curve has knee $\tmed(K')$ and
\[
  \min_{i\in T}K_i \;\le\; \tmed(K') \;\le\; \max_{i\in T}K_i .
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:commgen} supplies the knee; Theorem~\ref{thm:break} the interval.
\end{proof}

The corruption model is strong: the $k$ dishonest seeds may have entirely different curves;
monotonicity is assumed of them only so that they have knees.

\begin{theorem}[The mean pipeline has breakdown point zero]
For $N>1$, the curves $\sigma_1,\sigma_1,\sigma_N$ on $G=\{1,N\}$ at bar $1$ have two clean knees
equal to $1$, yet the knee of their mean curve is $N$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Exact stability ray]\label{thm:stab}
Let $b<c$. Then $\med_3(x,b,c) = b \iff x \le b$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $x\le b$ the sorted triple is $(x,b,c)$ with middle $b$; if $x>b$ then
$b < \min(x,c)\le\med_3(x,b,c)$.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:stab} is the sharp complement to Theorem~\ref{thm:break}: the interval in which
the centre is guaranteed to \emph{lie} and the set on which it is guaranteed to \emph{stay put}
are different objects.

\section{Application: a two-context, six-seed knee dataset}\label{sec:app}

The experiment: a causal word-level language model, vocabulary $4097$, held-out final $10\%$ of
the corpus, data-free top-$k$ attention selection, retention measured as accuracy at budget $k$
divided by full-attention accuracy, bar $0.98$.

\subsection{The measured sweep and its knee}

At $(d,\ctx)=(4,2048)$, seed $3$: full accuracy $0.1546$, bar $0.1516$, full loss $5.2199$. On the
grid $G=\{96,128,160,192,224,240,256,288,384,512,768,1024\}$:

\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccccccccccc}
\toprule
$k$ & 96 & 128 & 160 & 192 & 224 & 240 & 256 & 288 & 384 & 512 & 768 & 1024\\
\midrule
$c(k)$ & 0.963 & 0.973 & 0.981 & 0.984 & 0.986 & 0.987 & 0.990 & 0.993 & 0.999 & 1.000 & 1.003 & 1.003\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Writing the row as a base value plus nonnegative increments switched on at grid points makes
monotonicity structural rather than checked pointwise.

\begin{proposition}
The measured curve is non-decreasing and $\IsKnee_{G,0.98}(c,160)$, with margin
$c(160)-0.98 = 0.001$.
\end{proposition}

The margin is comparable to the binomial standard error of the accuracy measurement, so the
individual knee read is fragile -- exactly as the theory predicts for an extreme-order functional.
The recovery tail is well behaved: $c(512)=1.000$ and $c(768)=c(1024)=1.003$, a held-out loss
difference of $0.0016$.

\subsection{The horn analysis}

\begin{proposition}
Every $k \in \{192,224,240,256\}$ satisfies $c(k)\ge0.98$, and none is a knee of $c$ on $G$ at bar
$0.98$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The first claim is the sweep row; for the second, any knee is $\le 160$ by minimality applied to
the grid point $160$, which clears the bar.
\end{proof}

A prediction can be \emph{sound} (the budget really suffices) and yet \emph{not the answer} (it is
not the least sufficient budget). Sufficiency is an upper-bound claim; knee-hood is minimality.

\subsection{The $7/8$ median law}

Let $K_8=\{128,112,96\}$ with $P_8=128$, and $K_{16}=\{256,224,160\}$ with $P_{16}=256$.

\begin{proposition}
$\med(K_8) = 112 = \tfrac78 P_8$ and $\med(K_{16}) = 224 = \tfrac78 P_{16}$. Normalising by the
product point is order-preserving, so by Theorem~\ref{thm:mono}
$\med\{1,\tfrac78,\tfrac34\} = \tfrac78$ and $\med\{1,\tfrac78,\tfrac58\} = \tfrac78$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Uniqueness of the constant]
For $a\in\mathbb{Q}$: $aP_8 = 112$ and $aP_{16}=224$ iff $a = \tfrac78$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[The mean admits no such law]
There is no constant $a$ with $aP_8 = \tfrac{128+112+96}{3}$ and
$aP_{16} = \tfrac{256+224+160}{3}$: the first forces $a=\tfrac78$, the second $a=\tfrac56$.
\end{theorem}

This is the decisive discriminator between ``the centre satisfies a law'' and ``the median
satisfies a law'': the two summaries agree at one context and disagree at the other, and only one
extends.

\subsection{Speed-ups}

\begin{proposition}
At $\ctx=2048$ the speed-up multiset is $\{8,\,64/7,\,64/5\}\approx\{8.0,\,9.14,\,12.8\}$; by
Theorem~\ref{thm:anti} its median is $64/7 = 2048/224$. The least element $8$ is the image of the
\emph{largest} knee $256$ under order reversal (Proposition~\ref{prop:extreme}); every seed obeys
the product-law bound $k^{*}\le P_{16}$, so every seed deploys at at least $8\times$.
\end{proposition}

The deployment reading is therefore $\ge 8.0\times$ guaranteed, $9.1\times$ median, $12.8\times$
best: three order statistics of one distribution, of which only the middle is invariant under the
change of coordinates.

\subsection{Geometry of the two distributions}

\begin{proposition}
In normalised coordinates the profiles $(\min,\med,\max)$ are $(\tfrac34,\tfrac78,1)$ at
$\ctx=1024$ and $(\tfrac58,\tfrac78,1)$ at $\ctx=2048$. The upper edge is pinned at $1$; the
median is stationary at $7/8$; the lower tail strictly sinks; and the normalised spread grows by
exactly the factor $\tfrac{3/8}{1/4} = \tfrac32$.
\end{proposition}

The pinned ceiling is the product-law bound $k^{*}\le P$, verified for all six seeds; so the only
free coordinate as context grows is the lower tail, and the median -- the unique order statistic
equivariant under both $\div P$ and $\ctx/\cdot$ -- is the only summary insensitive to which
coordinate one reads.

\subsection{The exact stability region, and a corrected claim}

\begin{proposition}
$\med_3(x,224,256) = 224 \iff x \le 224$; in particular the family $\{160,192,224\}$ preserves the
reported centre.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}[A false informal claim]
``Only a third seed of $256$ or more would shift the median'' is false: $240<256$ yet
$\med_3(240,224,256) = 240 \ne 224$.
\end{proposition}

The stability region is the half-line $x \le 224$, whose endpoint is the current median, not the
interval $x<256$ delimited by the next data point. Theorem~\ref{thm:break} does guarantee that the
centre stays inside $[224,256]$ for any third value, but staying inside an interval and staying at
a point are different guarantees.

\subsection{The centre as an operating point}

\begin{proposition}
Any non-decreasing retention curves with knees $256,224,160$ have pointwise median curve of knee
exactly $224$; such curves exist (the unit steps $\sigma_{256},\sigma_{224},\sigma_{160}$ on
$G=\{160,224,256\}$ at bar $1$). With seeds $1$ and $2$ fixed and an arbitrary third measurement
$t$, the median curve has knee $\med_3(256,224,t)\in[224,256]$.
\end{proposition}

Thus ``the median knee is $224$'' is equivalent to ``the median of the three models, as a model,
needs a budget of $224$'', and no fourth measurement of the third seed can push that outside
$[224,256]$.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Knee detection.} Given a sorted grid $G=(g_1<\dots<g_n)$, a curve oracle and a bar,
return the least $g_i$ clearing the bar: $O(n)$ oracle calls, or $O(\log n)$ by binary search when
the curve is known monotone -- relevant when each call is an evaluation pass of a multi-hour
training run.

\paragraph{Median-curve knee.} Route A applies knee detection to each of $2k+1$ curves and takes
the median of the knees; Route B forms the pointwise median curve and applies knee detection once.
Theorem~\ref{thm:commgen} guarantees the two agree for monotone curves: Route A is cheaper, Route B
is what one defends as an operating point, and one need not choose.

\paragraph{Stability certificate.} For $2k+1$ seeds with one free coordinate, the reported centre
as a function of the free value is the non-decreasing step function $t\mapsto \med(K_{\text{pin}},t)$,
constant on $(-\infty,m]$ and on $[m',\infty)$ for the two central order statistics $m \le m'$ of
the pinned values; for three seeds this is Theorem~\ref{thm:stab}.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{Why point predictions failed and a structural prediction held}

A single knee is an extreme functional of a noisy curve, decided in this run by a margin of
$0.001$ against a comparable standard error. Nothing in the theory of the median predicts such a
quantity, and nothing should: knees are not $1$-Lipschitz functionals of the curve. The median, by
contrast, is majority-determined (Theorem~\ref{thm:dual}), invariant under monotone and antitone
changes of units (Theorems~\ref{thm:mono}--\ref{thm:anti}), $1$-Lipschitz (Theorem~\ref{thm:lip}),
immune to any minority of corrupted seeds (Theorem~\ref{thm:break}), and an operating point of a
genuine aggregate curve (Theorem~\ref{thm:comm3}). A prediction about it is a different kind of
claim from a prediction about a point, and the two should be scored separately.

\subsection{Honest limitations}

The seed-3 knee read is razor-thin ($+0.001$), so the true crossing lies between grid points,
plausibly near $150$--$160$. The sinking low tail is one measurement at the longer context; a
further seed would decide whether $5/8$ is stable or seed-specific. The $7/8$ law rests on two
contexts and six seeds; uniqueness of the constant makes it falsifiable, not confirmed. Measures
of attention concentration do \emph{not} sort with the measured knees across the three seeds at
the longer context, so there is no evidence here for a bounded ``working set'' explanation.
Finally, nothing here derives \emph{why} the centre should sit at $7/8$ of the product point.

\subsection{Relation to classical robust statistics}

That the median has breakdown point $1/2$ and the mean $0$ is classical. What is added is the
normal form making these algebraic identities in a semiring rather than analytic estimates; the
characterisation isolating translation equivariance as the property separating the median from
other conservative order-theoretic aggregators; and the commutation theorem, which concerns the
median not as a location estimator but as an aggregator commuting with a nonlinear, non-Lipschitz
read-out. The last has no analogue for the mean, and it is what licenses reporting a median knee
as an operating point rather than merely as a summary.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[The $7/8$ centre is a fixed point of context doubling]
For $\ctx_n = 2^n\ctx_0$ with $P_n = d\,\ctx_n/32$, the normalised three-seed knee distributions
$K_n/P_n$ have constant median $7/8$, strictly decreasing lower endpoints, and upper endpoints
pinned at $1$: the family is monotone in the lower tropical coordinate only. The pinned upper edge
is the product-law bound $k^{*}\le P$; the median, equivariant under both $\div P$ and
$\ctx/\cdot$, is the only summary insensitive to the coordinate read. A third context decides
whether the low tail follows a law or is noise.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Majority-threshold rigidity of the knee functional]
For monotone retention curves, curve $\mapsto$ knee is an order-reversing lattice homomorphism:
$\knee(c\wedge c') = \max(\knee c,\knee c')$ and $\knee(c\vee c') = \min(\knee c,\knee c')$, so
that every lattice polynomial in the curves commutes with the knee. Theorem~\ref{thm:comm3} is the
median instance; threshold duality converts any lattice polynomial into a monotone Boolean
function of the individual threshold events.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Phases of self-dual aggregators]
Among monotone, symmetric, translation-equivariant, self-dual ternary aggregators on $\mathbb{R}$,
exactly two phases occur: the conservative ones (all equal to the median, by
Theorem~\ref{thm:char}) and the strictly averaging ones, each of which fails some measured
two-context ratio law. Equivalently, no strictly averaging aggregator satisfies
$A(K_8) = \tfrac78P_8$ and $A(K_{16}) = \tfrac78P_{16}$ simultaneously.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Grid refinement and the continuum knee]
For curves continuous and strictly increasing near their crossing, the grid knee converges to the
continuum crossing as the grid refines, and the commutation theorem passes to the limit.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Quantitative stability under resampling]
Combining Theorem~\ref{thm:stab} with a noise model for individual knees yields the probability
that the reported centre changes under one further seed -- for the measured cell, the probability
that a fourth seed exceeds $224$.
\end{conjecture}

\section{Conclusion}

The median of an odd sample is a homogeneous tropical polynomial: the maximum, over all
$(k+1)$-subsets, of the minimum there. That normal form yields threshold duality, and threshold
duality yields everything else -- equivariance under monotone and antitone coordinate changes, an
axiomatic characterisation whose decisive axiom is the tropical one, nonexpansiveness, a majority
breakdown theorem, and the exact commutation of median aggregation with knee reading, which the
arithmetic mean fails outright. Applied to a measured attention-budget dataset, the theory derives
the knee $k^{*}=160$ and its razor-thin margin, shows the four pre-registered predictions to be
simultaneously sound and non-minimal, establishes that the two-context median law
$\med(K) = \tfrac78 P$ has a unique constant and no mean-based analogue, identifies the pinned
ceiling as the product-law bound and the sinking floor as the sole free coordinate, and pins the
stability region of the reported centre to the ray $x\le224$.

When the quantity you can measure is fragile and the quantity you want to predict is structural,
choose the summary whose algebra commutes with your read-out. In the threshold-crossing setting,
that summary is the median, and the reason is tropical.

